<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Itanos</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Itanos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Port city of far eastern Crete, involved in the founding of Cyrene and long locked in disputes over the sanctuary of Diktaean Zeus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Itanos lay on a bay at the north-east tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, near Cape Sideros, and was settled in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, possibly with Phoenician involvement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells that Corobius, a purple-fisher from Itanos, guided the Therans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> before they founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cyrene">Cyrene</a> c. 631 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Itanos lived from fishing, purple dye and trade, with coins showing sea creatures. It had a long-running dispute with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/praisos">Praisos</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hierapytna">Hierapytna</a> over the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diktaean-zeus">Diktaean Zeus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaikastro">Palaikastro</a> and the land around it. In the 3rd century BCE it hosted a Ptolemaic garrison, which protected it against its neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> arbitrated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hierapytna">Hierapytna</a> dispute through Magnesia c. 112 BCE. Itanos was absorbed into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> after 67 BCE and was abandoned in the Byzantine period.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/itanos.jpg" alt="Itanos" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Vadim Indeikin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%92%D0%B8%D0%B4_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%BC%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D1%8B%D1%80%D1%8C_%D1%81%D0%BE_%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D1%8F%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B8._Toplou._Lasithi._Crete._Greece._%D0%90%D0%B2%D0%B3%D1%83%D1%81%D1%82_2013_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Itanos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Diktaean Zeus</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Itanus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=itanos" aria-label="Itanos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5344 905 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Itanos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5664 1161L5772 1297M5664 1161L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5772 1297h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5664 1161h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5664 1161h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=itanos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

